IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `tfm-dates`
IMPORT `tfm-rules`

-- Testator's Family Maintenance Act 1912 (Tas) -- the questions of the CLAIMANT.
--
-- SEAT: the person who claims provision out of the estate of a person who has died -- the spouse or partner, a child
-- (adopted, step- or surrogate child included), a parent where no spouse or child was left, or a former spouse or
-- partner who was receiving maintenance: "Can I claim?", "By when must I take out my summons?", "Will the Court hear me
-- if I file on this day?", "Can I still ask for more time?", "Can the Court give me provision, and in what form?", "Can
-- it refuse me for my conduct?", "Can the executor apply for my child?", "What estate does the Court look at?", "Does my
-- provision end if I remarry?", "Must I be told if someone applies to change my order?". The executor or administrator
-- (who acts for the estate), the Court and the judge get no seat: their findings are asked as facts. The beneficiary of
-- the estate who answers the claim has a seat of his or her own (tfm-seat-beneficiary.l4).
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, dates, numbers, a choice from a list) and calls the rules of tfm-rules.l4.
-- One DEONTIC rule (the three months after the grant, s 11(1)) is run inside the questions that need it; no question
-- takes a DEONTIC or an EVENT.
--
-- UNOFFICIAL. Based on material from the Tasmanian Legislation website at 6 November 2026 (c) State of Tasmania, CC BY
-- 4.0; not the authorised version. See SOURCE-LICENSE.md. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Testator's Family Maintenance Act 1912 (Tas): the claimant's questions`

§§ `Who may claim`

@export Can I apply for provision out of the estate of the person who died (Testator's Family Maintenance Act 1912 (Tas) ss 2, 3A)? Yes for their spouse (by marriage, or a partner in a significant relationship with them when they died), their child (including an adopted child, a surrogate child, and -- where they died on or after 13 October 2015 -- a stepchild), a parent if they left no spouse or child, and a former spouse or former partner who was receiving or entitled to receive maintenance from them when they died. A parent is excluded where they left a spouse or child. The Act names no one else; for a stepchild where they died earlier the answer depends on the earlier definition, which is not encoded.
GIVEN `what was my relationship to the person who died` IS A `Relationship to the deceased` @desc Your relationship to the person who died: spouse by marriage; partner in a significant relationship at the death (Relationships Act 2003); child; adopted child; child of the deceased's spouse; child whose natural parent was the deceased's spouse when that parent died; surrogate child (Surrogacy Act 2012); parent; former spouse, the marriage dissolved or annulled; former partner, the significant relationship ended before the death; or another relationship
      `did they leave a spouse or any children` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only for a parent: whether the person who died left a spouse (or partner) or any children surviving them
      `was I receiving or entitled to receive maintenance from them when they died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only for a former spouse or former partner: whether, at the date of the death, you were receiving or entitled to receive maintenance from the person who died, under a court order, an agreement or otherwise
      `the day of the death` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I apply for provision out of the estate` MEANS
    `s 3A -- I may apply for provision` `what was my relationship to the person who died` `did they leave a spouse or any children` `was I receiving or entitled to receive maintenance from them when they died` `the day of the death`

@export Can the executor or administrator apply for provision on my behalf because I am under age or lack mental capacity (Testator's Family Maintenance Act 1912 (Tas) s 3(5))? Yes if you may apply under s 3A and are not of full age or mental capacity: the executor or administrator may apply for you, or ask the Court for directions on whether to. Anyone may also apply "on behalf of" a person who may apply (s 3A).
GIVEN `what was my relationship to the person who died` IS A `Relationship to the deceased` @desc Your relationship to the person who died: spouse by marriage; partner in a significant relationship at the death (Relationships Act 2003); child; adopted child; child of the deceased's spouse; child whose natural parent was the deceased's spouse when that parent died; surrogate child (Surrogacy Act 2012); parent; former spouse, the marriage dissolved or annulled; former partner, the significant relationship ended before the death; or another relationship
      `did they leave a spouse or any children` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only for a parent: whether the person who died left a spouse (or partner) or any children surviving them
      `was I receiving or entitled to receive maintenance from them when they died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only for a former spouse or former partner: whether, at the date of the death, you were receiving or entitled to receive maintenance from the person who died, under a court order, an agreement or otherwise
      `the day of the death` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died
      `am I under age or without mental capacity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are not of full age (under 18) or not of full mental capacity
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the executor apply on my behalf` MEANS
    `s 3(5) -- the executor may apply on my behalf` (`s 3A -- I may apply for provision` `what was my relationship to the person who died` `did they leave a spouse or any children` `was I receiving or entitled to receive maintenance from them when they died` `the day of the death`) `am I under age or without mental capacity`

§§ `The time limit`

@export By when must I take out my summons to claim provision out of the estate (Testator's Family Maintenance Act 1912 (Tas) s 11(1))? The summons in chambers must be taken out before, or not later than three months after, the date of the grant of probate of the will or of letters of administration. Before the grant, you may take it out at any time. Three months after a grant on the 30th or 31st of a month ends on the last day of a shorter month. The Court may extend the time if you apply before the estate is finally distributed (s 11(2)-(4)).
GIVEN `has probate or letters of administration been granted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether probate of the will, or letters of administration of the estate, have been granted
      `the day of the grant` IS A DATE @desc The day probate or letters of administration were granted (any date if not yet granted)
GIVETH A `Summons deadline`
`By when must I take out my summons` MEANS
    `s 11(1) -- by when I must take out the summons` `has probate or letters of administration been granted` `the day of the grant`

@export Can the Court hear my application if my summons is taken out on this day (Testator's Family Maintenance Act 1912 (Tas) ss 3(4), 11)? Yes if the summons is taken out before the grant of probate or letters of administration or within three months after it; if the Court has extended the time to a day on or after it; or if the Court has ordered that another person's in-time application be treated as made by all who may apply. Otherwise the Court has no jurisdiction to hear it.
GIVEN `has probate or letters of administration been granted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether probate of the will, or letters of administration of the estate, had been granted by the day of your summons
      `the day of the grant` IS A DATE @desc The day probate or letters of administration were granted (any date if not granted)
      `the day of my summons` IS A DATE @desc The day your summons in chambers is (or would be) taken out
      `has another in-time application been ordered to be treated as made by everyone entitled` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has ordered (s 3(4)) that another person's application, taken out in time, be regarded as an application on behalf of everyone entitled to apply
      `has the Court extended the time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court or a judge has extended the time for taking out your summons (s 11(2))
      `the last day of the extended time` IS A DATE @desc The last day of the time as extended (any date if not extended)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the Court hear my application if I take out the summons on this day` MEANS
    `s 11 -- the Court has jurisdiction to hear my application` `has probate or letters of administration been granted` `the day of the grant` `the day of my summons` `has another in-time application been ordered to be treated as made by everyone entitled` `has the Court extended the time` `the last day of the extended time`

@export Can I still apply to the Court for more time to take out my summons (Testator's Family Maintenance Act 1912 (Tas) s 11(2)-(4))? Yes, even after the three months have run out, as long as you apply before the final distribution of the estate; after the final distribution, no. Whether to extend, and for how long, is for the Court, and nothing distributed before you apply can be disturbed.
GIVEN `has the estate been finally distributed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the final distribution of the estate of the person who died has been made
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I still apply for more time` MEANS
    `s 11(2)-(4) -- I may apply for an extension of time` `has the estate been finally distributed`

§§ `The provision the Court may order`

@export Can the Court order provision for me out of the estate (Testator's Family Maintenance Act 1912 (Tas) ss 3(1), 3A, 11)? Yes, in its discretion, if you may apply, the Court has jurisdiction (your summons was in time or the time was extended), and you were left by the will or the intestacy without adequate provision for your proper maintenance and support. The Court may still refuse for your character or conduct (s 8(1)), and it looks at the net estate and your other means (s 7).
GIVEN `what was my relationship to the person who died` IS A `Relationship to the deceased` @desc Your relationship to the person who died: spouse by marriage; partner in a significant relationship at the death (Relationships Act 2003); child; adopted child; child of the deceased's spouse; child whose natural parent was the deceased's spouse when that parent died; surrogate child (Surrogacy Act 2012); parent; former spouse, the marriage dissolved or annulled; former partner, the significant relationship ended before the death; or another relationship
      `did they leave a spouse or any children` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only for a parent: whether the person who died left a spouse (or partner) or any children surviving them
      `was I receiving or entitled to receive maintenance from them when they died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only for a former spouse or former partner: whether, at the date of the death, you were receiving or entitled to receive maintenance from the person who died, under a court order, an agreement or otherwise
      `the day of the death` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died
      `has probate or letters of administration been granted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether probate of the will, or letters of administration of the estate, had been granted by the day of your summons
      `the day of the grant` IS A DATE @desc The day probate or letters of administration were granted (any date if not granted)
      `the day of my summons` IS A DATE @desc The day your summons in chambers is (or would be) taken out
      `has another in-time application been ordered to be treated as made by everyone entitled` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has ordered (s 3(4)) that another person's application, taken out in time, be regarded as an application on behalf of everyone entitled to apply
      `has the Court extended the time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court or a judge has extended the time for taking out your summons (s 11(2))
      `the last day of the extended time` IS A DATE @desc The last day of the time as extended (any date if not extended)
      `was I left without adequate provision for my proper maintenance and support` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, by the will or the intestacy, you were left without adequate provision for your proper maintenance and support, as the Court would find it
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the Court order provision for me out of the estate` MEANS
    `s 3(1) -- the Court may order provision for me` (`s 3A -- I may apply for provision` `what was my relationship to the person who died` `did they leave a spouse or any children` `was I receiving or entitled to receive maintenance from them when they died` `the day of the death`) (`s 11 -- the Court has jurisdiction to hear my application` `has probate or letters of administration been granted` `the day of the grant` `the day of my summons` `has another in-time application been ordered to be treated as made by everyone entitled` `has the Court extended the time` `the last day of the extended time`) `was I left without adequate provision for my proper maintenance and support`

@export Can the Court refuse my application because of my character or conduct (Testator's Family Maintenance Act 1912 (Tas) s 8(1))? Yes if, in the Court's opinion, your character or conduct should disentitle you to the benefit of any provision under the Act; otherwise s 8(1) gives no ground to refuse (the Court keeps its general discretion under s 3(1)).
GIVEN `is the Court of the opinion my character or conduct should disentitle me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court or judge is of the opinion that your character or conduct is such that it should disentitle you to any provision
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the Court refuse me for my character or conduct` MEANS
    `s 8(1) -- the Court may refuse for character or conduct` `is the Court of the opinion my character or conduct should disentitle me`

@export What is the net value of the estate that the Court looks at in deciding my claim (Testator's Family Maintenance Act 1912 (Tas) s 7(a))? The gross value of the estate less all debts, testamentary and funeral expenses, and all other lawful liabilities of the estate (never below zero). The Court also looks at your independent means (s 7(b)).
GIVEN `the gross value of the estate` IS A NUMBER @desc The gross value of the estate of the person who died, in dollars
      `the debts of the estate` IS A NUMBER @desc All the debts of the estate, in dollars
      `the testamentary and funeral expenses` IS A NUMBER @desc The testamentary expenses (of proving the will and administering the estate) and the funeral expenses, in dollars
      `the other lawful liabilities of the estate` IS A NUMBER @desc All other lawful liabilities the estate is subject to, in dollars
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the net value of the estate` MEANS
    `s 7(a) -- the net value of the estate` `the gross value of the estate` `the debts of the estate` `the testamentary and funeral expenses` `the other lawful liabilities of the estate`

@export Can the Court make my provision in this form (Testator's Family Maintenance Act 1912 (Tas) s 3(2), (3))? Yes for a lump sum; a life interest or lesser interest in a dwelling-house of the estate; or a life interest or lesser interest in a dwelling-house the Court orders to be bought for you to live in (with estate money, whatever the will says). The Court may attach terms and conditions. For another form, s 3(2) does not say, though s 3(1) lets the Court order such provision as it thinks proper.
GIVEN `the form of provision` IS A `Form of provision` @desc The form of provision: a lump sum; a life interest or lesser interest in a dwelling-house of the estate; a life interest or lesser interest in a dwelling-house bought for me to live in; or another form
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my provision take this form` MEANS
    `s 3(2) -- the provision may take this form` `the form of provision`

§§ `After an order`
-- Arises only once the Court has made an order providing for you.

@export If I remarry or enter a significant relationship, does my provision as the spouse continue (Testator's Family Maintenance Act 1912 (Tas) s 9(4))? Yes where the Court's order, made for you as the spouse of the person who died, directs that the provision continues despite a later remarriage or significant relationship. Otherwise the Act does not say: the order's own terms decide.
GIVEN `what was my relationship to the person who died` IS A `Relationship to the deceased` @desc Your relationship to the person who died: spouse by marriage; partner in a significant relationship at the death (Relationships Act 2003); child; adopted child; child of the deceased's spouse; child whose natural parent was the deceased's spouse when that parent died; surrogate child (Surrogacy Act 2012); parent; former spouse, the marriage dissolved or annulled; former partner, the significant relationship ended before the death; or another relationship
      `does my order direct that it continues despite remarriage` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the order making provision for you directs that it operates for your benefit even if you later remarry or enter into a significant relationship
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does my provision continue if I remarry` MEANS
    `s 9(4) -- my provision continues if I remarry` (`s 9(4) -- I am the spouse` `what was my relationship to the person who died`) `does my order direct that it continues despite remarriage`

@export Must I be served with notice if someone applies to rescind or alter the order (Testator's Family Maintenance Act 1912 (Tas) s 9(5), (6))? Yes if you take any benefit under the order: the executor, administrator or beneficiary who applies must have notice of the application served on you. The Court may rescind the order or alter it, increasing or reducing provision, at any time.
GIVEN `do I take a benefit under the order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you take any benefit under the order the application seeks to rescind or alter
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I be served with notice of an application to change the order` MEANS
    `s 9(6) -- I must be served with notice of the application` `do I take a benefit under the order`
